#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"}] :question/id #uuid "8ddc5edb-0181-50c7-9d1a-8ee206f1c08b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6773" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\dfrac{3(2)^{kn}}{4^n}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078266 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"}] :question/id #uuid "e623670b-6132-5b91-bd5e-4a52716f1d8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-689" :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n+1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2+1}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| <1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078268 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/id #uuid "a8b61e74-1a6d-5efb-bda9-f91aca63d0d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-691" :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty {3}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{n}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}}{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078269 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/id #uuid "454e2614-06fe-5fcd-b9cd-856e544f7d1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-693" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(7)}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{28}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{24}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d66ccb8-2ea5-5bc7-9089-c99a081b58f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293456} {:answer/id #uuid "e610fd6d-7a4a-5ec3-bea9-8c0eeb50e3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293457} {:answer/id #uuid "6547c543-b8e0-58cd-9b7e-25a87d3b1588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293458} {:answer/id #uuid "de2f55fd-0fcf-5153-9a86-9d7c0af13b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293459} {:answer/id #uuid "a6779718-0a71-557c-a7a7-7a7318a26897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e610fd6d-7a4a-5ec3-bea9-8c0eeb50e3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293457} :answer-field/id #uuid "172b9b5e-0fc9-5296-bd96-ffb4a8568039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3f4eb83-13db-5ea2-8282-93f1520d91c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124900" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(-0.3)}^{n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=4.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(-0.3)}^{5}}{{(-0.3)}^{4}} \\\\  & =-0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3640d6c4-1970-5f74-b53b-afeb11bce18e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293463} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c7e8dd-6e97-5ff6-a92f-77781bd5b8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293464} {:answer/id #uuid "59c1ded3-6917-52cc-a662-ff2e463df991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293465} {:answer/id #uuid "3790dfc7-0b7b-5126-8cdd-32eac099c5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293466} {:answer/id #uuid "f648ace9-dc1a-5d60-b8c2-d5cc0da66721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1c7e8dd-6e97-5ff6-a92f-77781bd5b8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293464} :answer-field/id #uuid "572aa021-4f31-50e3-a92e-0460a1c0fb21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bc831f8-da9e-536b-8c11-db2b723d1c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-124901" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }1.5{(0.8)}^{n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=4.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5{(0.8)}^{5}}{1.5{(0.8)}^{4}} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1545e8be-5b17-5a36-ba02-42d585c8d7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293470} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fe9fa4-c087-561a-9b03-af80dd4596dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293471} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c76799-a70c-5584-85ee-1ef29cd2d0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293472} {:answer/id #uuid "7a312761-0d12-5707-8550-79fa40f0f747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293473} {:answer/id #uuid "246fa7bf-24dc-570b-860f-4875fb3c7abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7c76799-a70c-5584-85ee-1ef29cd2d0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293472} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f040b7-a861-517f-b2cc-87b018b09d90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0860410e-f60a-5b80-a1c8-6a3d047379c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124906" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{0.25{(1.1)}^{1}}{0.25{(1.1)}^{0}} \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$ starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{3-1}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{3}^{2-1}}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db5356e-50e1-59b3-bb3e-b2f75abf8053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293477} {:answer/id #uuid "852359ed-8cf2-5bf5-a3f5-621916fc8cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293478} {:answer/id #uuid "c68a442b-2ecc-57ac-a851-f52e0b1bf81f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293479} {:answer/id #uuid "c59ce3d3-43c6-5524-8cd0-b01523e025ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293480} {:answer/id #uuid "f398900c-0757-548b-931c-76d1a6a4dd7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f398900c-0757-548b-931c-76d1a6a4dd7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293481} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9be666-3755-56fd-b7a4-bfdfe407b825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55c1789a-3f1b-5ebc-a34c-eaab270f436c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124912" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $-1+1-1+1-⋯$\n- $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$\n- $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-1+1-1+1-⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.11}{0.1} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent  because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "329bacbe-75a0-50d3-bb78-97fd3c6bb3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293484} {:answer/id #uuid "11de28c4-6734-5cbf-aa46-c4221ef7716c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293485} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb81f44-1ce5-56b9-874f-08a1b68ef5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293486} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc1d7ac-3d06-5834-b298-e6dbcd219807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293487} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2171e5-28ec-54b5-8a7b-c94c7d7509a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccc1d7ac-3d06-5834-b298-e6dbcd219807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293487} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb2a0ad3-7172-5af3-bbae-a6ab9da2e6ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3040a9b-f07a-5c90-9674-eff18efde317" :question/math-academy-id "q-124924" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn}}{{100}^{n}\\cdot 10} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{10}^{k}}{100} & <1 \\\\ {10}^{k} & <100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${10}^{k}=100$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${10}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${10}^{k}<100$ must be $k<2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a08c2b63-9d2c-5fb8-a648-1fb3fa981f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a08c2b63-9d2c-5fb8-a648-1fb3fa981f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293491} :answer-field/id #uuid "d122179f-1932-53fe-8be2-63639107c3bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293490} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f95d9359-367d-58b0-877e-b28a1a26a24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f95d9359-367d-58b0-877e-b28a1a26a24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293493} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5035434-8efb-5412-b0b1-0c248819ba24" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc4ec5a6-b7f3-5cda-ba58-721e08ead6c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-347229" :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{3}^{8}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{9}^{4}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{k-4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{k-4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{k-4}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{k-4} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{k-4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{k-4}=1$ has the solution $k=4.$ Now, since ${9}^{k-4}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{k-4}<1$ must be $k<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d09fcad5-7e81-5683-ac5f-6276d370cdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d09fcad5-7e81-5683-ac5f-6276d370cdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293496} :answer-field/id #uuid "68efd871-a33d-5d79-b6cb-f1f1b6cd78c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293495} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72bfe6b0-cdc8-5fd8-bd3d-16c044449988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72bfe6b0-cdc8-5fd8-bd3d-16c044449988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293498} :answer-field/id #uuid "43761fe4-137b-560a-90f3-85cd13ed7030" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ab273945-3eda-5439-90ad-bb1b762ef6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-351372" :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{3}^{6}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{3-k})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{3-k}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{3-k}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{3-k} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{3-k}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{3-k}=1$ has the solution $k=3.$ Now, since ${9}^{3-k}$ decreases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{3-k}<1$ must be $k>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0241e30-571a-57e8-85ec-c3da54e0bf25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293501} {:answer/id #uuid "34a61084-acd4-589b-8839-64c470216664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293502} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec4768f-02f9-5a25-811b-e7d96245f0ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293503} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa5e743-3f97-55c6-b08b-0ca93f9c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293504} {:answer/id #uuid "6367f161-92ea-5f78-9e9e-4fb23c5ec152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa5e743-3f97-55c6-b08b-0ca93f9c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293504} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7bdef6-6639-5f43-8df8-c7f88578dc0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d6af0ce-2d7e-5e7e-9759-710fbc03ce1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40277" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$\n- $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$\n- $-8+16-32+64-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-2}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-8+16-32+64-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79e5acd7-dd7e-5ea8-88ee-185b6a91f49c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293508} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ae32b0-2901-5a06-b135-c9e2469a7011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293509} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed13411-0552-58fe-9b75-e8244ef889f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293510} {:answer/id #uuid "e98ce386-ad06-5e85-9a9f-5398d8037b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293511} {:answer/id #uuid "bfaca0e9-18da-5f11-b0c7-2c8b07d86b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e98ce386-ad06-5e85-9a9f-5398d8037b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293511} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf08d0d-3b07-51af-a637-5f9df8c367d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ba97993-eea9-588a-a8e1-7812184f7fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40282" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $1+5+25+125+⋯$\n- $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$\n- $0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $1+5+25+125+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.01}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series III converges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8c4a0a-bdc8-54de-8a7e-923192e070db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293515} {:answer/id #uuid "8a899d68-5663-59a6-8c0c-0fd81fc4d647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293516} {:answer/id #uuid "6572a204-4d15-550a-bc38-600634155f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293517} {:answer/id #uuid "116345f6-7b08-56b2-ac90-5963ae1da95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293518} {:answer/id #uuid "3882ed75-8528-56aa-9022-c68352e9e90e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a899d68-5663-59a6-8c0c-0fd81fc4d647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293516} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb45e08-2da7-5726-a9ea-50900f541d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecdef7ff-1e8e-5448-88b3-81c3a4f39ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40579" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3(2)}}{0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{6}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72006af8-0884-50ae-8230-31a2f35ec785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293522} {:answer/id #uuid "2eacd0ef-e87c-5bde-97f9-10754c0981e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293523} {:answer/id #uuid "9f19568d-1592-5313-a80e-867ed1e4e289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293524} {:answer/id #uuid "d2daebe2-6ccd-5ba6-bc3b-d477dd584e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293525} {:answer/id #uuid "203072a5-292e-5d66-b2e2-a3fdb36c2cc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2daebe2-6ccd-5ba6-bc3b-d477dd584e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293525} :answer-field/id #uuid "905900f1-a590-5906-8f97-88410f694965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed54a7f7-e913-5b8a-b062-8a7b79cd167c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40590" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn}\\cdot 16}{{4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(16\\cdot 4){(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }64{(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{16}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {16}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${16}^{k}=4$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${16}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${16}^{k}<4$ must be $k<\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa027fd0-1806-51a4-b983-b44bb63fbf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293529} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cf2cd0-dacb-5072-804c-7e4931260523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293530} {:answer/id #uuid "17157c03-4542-532f-a9ca-c898ab12a219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293531} {:answer/id #uuid "682245a4-841b-57e9-ac3d-21e306e62736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293532} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1268ad-3b1f-5f67-8608-04384cdb6a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5cf2cd0-dacb-5072-804c-7e4931260523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293530} :answer-field/id #uuid "d891d2c0-634f-55a3-9528-6139a95c51eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f576e449-3f8d-544f-ad07-4e21e895f136" :question/math-academy-id "q-50506" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})}{(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1})(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  I and III converge."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99f7266-1916-5eea-8814-ac71b6419631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293536} {:answer/id #uuid "24883e73-c34b-568b-88b8-1a41f74c9687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293537} {:answer/id #uuid "7dab21ce-fead-548b-bd76-368880f8ecf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293538} {:answer/id #uuid "b26f33f4-1a44-557c-84e6-02917dc0aa5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293539} {:answer/id #uuid "dc295d73-8ad5-5adb-b004-aeb20c04a493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24883e73-c34b-568b-88b8-1a41f74c9687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293537} :answer-field/id #uuid "1689ec89-5882-5c9b-aac1-20820533d151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4f0938e-d364-50f7-854f-2c7d8c9bb244" :question/math-academy-id "q-5457" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{7{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}{7{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a8120a-6c01-5abe-aa2f-a009e8ce9057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293543} {:answer/id #uuid "ed295b09-479f-56c4-a027-786a2b43a2d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293544} {:answer/id #uuid "60168973-624c-5657-b58f-f57ecf5531bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293545} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2cb9d2-845d-535e-bc82-26fe67d3735e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293546} {:answer/id #uuid "617f9be5-addb-5e7d-8598-9a841b7eae98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "617f9be5-addb-5e7d-8598-9a841b7eae98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293547} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f1da19-6222-5968-b6ae-d912b7469533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa207fa2-662b-5e6f-a5ac-1dc566596197" :question/math-academy-id "q-67373" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{6}{(-1)}^{n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{6}{(-1)}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{6}{(-1)}^{1}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a591aa84-ac21-5c85-9e67-da9390866c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293550} {:answer/id #uuid "ec760539-1bee-5953-9c3d-a237ee76029a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\le 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293551} {:answer/id #uuid "3e63526a-a480-57ac-b839-d3ec5640e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293552} {:answer/id #uuid "8eeb96de-6c22-5fba-9f27-f4d9aadf80c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293553} {:answer/id #uuid "869da3b1-c41d-51d7-bcac-a2e60486d446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "869da3b1-c41d-51d7-bcac-a2e60486d446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293554} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3083999-de29-5a98-bbfa-0ad28a5028c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b03216e8-33f4-51f9-bb85-457599267180" :question/math-academy-id "q-67458" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{5}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n+1}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=3.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4+1}}{\\frac{3}{5}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{5}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb99ac16-a789-55e0-8d82-af6105baac03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293557} {:answer/id #uuid "862edc4d-2c9f-59a7-a949-fe94876348a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293558} {:answer/id #uuid "90bb58e7-77d8-5247-92bd-e7b62d507731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293559} {:answer/id #uuid "012bf300-8fd3-566a-b3c6-238f74278099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293560} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8faee2-fcb6-51f9-b212-54e7bf50e3f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862edc4d-2c9f-59a7-a949-fe94876348a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293558} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41a10c5-0b50-59cf-8fd5-5e9e3d941fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c53384c-4d83-5794-87e6-e6659870884d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67459" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series \n ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2(1)}}{(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2(0)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd43644-ad19-56df-af2d-cd12fc91f6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293564} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b66e86-e63c-5acd-9f55-8a8bac69c24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293565} {:answer/id #uuid "06bee343-6049-5be6-a490-4e59e91158d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293566} {:answer/id #uuid "46b80352-8a5c-5e75-86d6-1e17a7220f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293567} {:answer/id #uuid "8e415ae7-32eb-5ad0-897f-20abf00424e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|>1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd43644-ad19-56df-af2d-cd12fc91f6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293564} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf019b9b-29c7-52e2-9f28-bb19ee47d2d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38d70bc0-103b-5ba6-820b-4a5aed804138" :question/math-academy-id "q-78480" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(0.6)^{n+1}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{3{(0.6)}^{2+1}}{3{(0.6)}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{(0.6)}^{3}}{3{(0.6)}^{2}} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abff1e9a-91e2-56a1-a48c-059220e59a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293571} {:answer/id #uuid "63f843fa-9b5b-5c0e-b0b9-1d495dea740b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293572} {:answer/id #uuid "716bb8f0-1ea5-5f55-943e-2ec74c2647b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293573} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a08238-78ae-594f-b47c-b95afb0f6cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293574} {:answer/id #uuid "511e0d61-8ad3-513b-9b7d-95a5dad1753a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f843fa-9b5b-5c0e-b0b9-1d495dea740b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293572} :answer-field/id #uuid "96273cd4-93f1-5e56-9259-d97525f1196f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4491981d-ece3-5014-843e-910429266c33" :question/math-academy-id "q-78485" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3}{(-3)}^{n+1}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}{(-3)}^{2+1}}{\\frac{2}{3}{(-3)}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd740cce-fd78-5964-9b72-99691f7326ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293578} {:answer/id #uuid "16a8aba4-b642-59eb-9ee8-cc11dfcdadfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293579} {:answer/id #uuid "5f64c84b-d68e-50e0-975d-c380b0ff143e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293580} {:answer/id #uuid "9efa792b-2dbf-5d91-9aaf-b84dc810bd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|>1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293581} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fd6bde-199c-5a36-8644-8c31c443d32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a8aba4-b642-59eb-9ee8-cc11dfcdadfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293579} :answer-field/id #uuid "f66d5287-14be-5106-8562-3c032c98ef24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9c218b5-4394-55c2-957a-729e341c048f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78487" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{3}}{\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "485e2466-bab6-583d-8937-9cdcaa9ffc28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293585} {:answer/id #uuid "6e176fd3-77ad-588d-b032-de5593b426f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293586} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae7667e-cde9-5b73-abc7-f634c3c86a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293587} {:answer/id #uuid "3966187a-4f68-5137-a0b3-c5c205f93706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293588} {:answer/id #uuid "d56cb2ce-34f7-5626-b702-4694d3f4929c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d56cb2ce-34f7-5626-b702-4694d3f4929c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293589} :answer-field/id #uuid "693ba853-1694-52f1-a34d-8fcf382a81b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81fee5bd-1991-5407-ab65-90b191c16e7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78490" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}})}^{n}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "We see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{64}{{2}^{k}} & <1 \\\\ 64 & <{2}^{k} \\\\ {2}^{k} & >64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${2}^{k}=64$ has the solution $k=6.$ Now, since ${2}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${2}^{k}>64$ must be $k>6.$"}]}
